A||||2||Robert||Another gas mileage question?||||5||
Z||000000||Robert||04-08-2000||11:34 AM||||Here is a gas mileage question my wife and I have wondered about during our trips to the mountains in our 91 Toyota truck (I imagine this would be the same in a Fiero).<BR>Here is the question; while driving up a steep grade at 55 mph, what would give me the best gas mileage, foot to the floor in 5th or half throttle in 4th?  <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ed||04-08-2000||02:15 PM||||Install a vacuum gauge and drive to keep the vacuum in the low (economy) range. <P>I'd say highest (like 5th) gear without completely lugging or overheating the engine.  <P>Just get out of my way when I want to pass in my Fiero!<P>Incidentally, I got 42 mpg in my sprite in an economy run many years ago.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-08-2000||05:37 PM||||Rob,<P>The lower gear is likely more efficient.<P>Using the vac gauge like Ed said should help confirm it.<P>The general idea is that every engine has an RPM range where it really want to be run for fuel economy and another one for max torque and yet another for max HP.<P>If you have to floor it to climb in 5 then drop a gear and use the tranny for what it was made for. <P>The trick is keeping the engine in the RPM range you need it in for what your doing. 5th wasn't ment to climb it's usually an overdriven gear. 4th in most trannys is either straight thru or a very slight gearing down. 1,2,3 are all gear reductions in 4 or 5 speeds.<P>So in your case it can't climb worth a damn in 5. That should tell you to drop a gear. Same thing around town. Most 5 speeds I've driven can't be run above 3rd or 4th in city driving.||||reg||1
